      "snippet": {
        "text": "In the realm of PBL, the comprehensive summary and assessment stage holds a paramount position in cultivating learners' creative thinking. This phase, marked by its emphasis on feedback and evaluation, is not merely a procedural step but a critical component in the educational journey, significantly influencing the development of creativity. \n\nFeedback and evaluation in this context are more than just tools for assessment; they are catalysts for fostering a deeper understanding and enhancement of creative skills. According to Davis and Dargusch [33], feedback provides an opportunity for students to develop assessment literacy, which is crucial for their growth as self-regulated learners. This self-regulation is a cornerstone in nurturing creativity, as it empowers students to take control of their learning processes. Nicol and Macfarlane-Dick [34] further emphasize the importance of feedback in promoting iterative learning processes, which are essential for the development of creative thinking. \n\nThe comprehensive summary and assessment stage in PBL encompasses various forms of feedback, each playing a unique role in enhancing creativity. Teacher evaluations, as Hattie and Timperley [35]note, provide professional and authoritative insights, guiding learners in understanding their innovative thinking and project performance. Peer assessments, as highlighted by Van den Berg [36], offer diverse perspectives, enabling learners to view their creative processes through different lenses. This multiplicity of viewpoints enriches the creative experience. Furthermore, self-assessment, as Black and William [37]argue, fosters self-reflection and metacognitive abilities, which are vital for the development of innovative thinking. \n\nFeedback in the educational setting is not just about imparting information on performance; it is about creating an experience that fosters hope and empowerment. Fossey [38] point out that effective feedback practices make the tacit dimensions of students' knowledge explicit, guiding them through problem-solving phases and fostering creativity. Öhman and Tanner [39]supports this view, emphasizing that feedback is a guiding tool that helps students navigate their learning journey. \n\nMoreover, feedback on performance assessment tasks is a key driver for improvement and progress in student learning achievement. Orrell [40]notes that feedback encourages 'deep' learning, where students value tutors' comments and actively seek feedback that helps them engage deeply with their subject matter. This deep engagement is a critical factor in fostering creative thinking.",
