        }
      },
      "snippet": {
        "text": "Peer assessment enhances student learning outcomes, develops their interpersonal skills, builds learner autonomy and motivation and can improve student performance across different education levels, subject areas, and assessment types. Existing frameworks and typologies adequately cover the range of design decisions that educators should consider when seeking to implement effective peer assessment processes. However, educators cannot develop assessment of such processes through 'selecting branches of a decision tree that lead to an optimal outcome'; instead, they must reflect on a range of 'assessment considerations' that 'underpin the designs available within the constraints of their own environment' (Bearman et al., 2016, p. 551). \n\nWe find that peer assessment works best as formative assessment providing opportunities for students to build feedback literacy. We find strong evidence that providing students with training, clear assessment criteria and appropriate task scaffolding; allowing for multiple iterations of peer assessment; clearly explaining upfront the benefits of peer assessment and how it aligns with other assessment and learning outcomes; combining peer assessment with self-assessment; building in assessment flexibility; enabling collaborative assessment (where feasible); and supporting teachers with necessary resources, policy shifts and cultural change can lead to enhanced learning outcomes across a range of contexts. We find less clear evidence relating to anonymity, online v offline settings, peer marking, and peer matching.",
