      "snippet": {
        "text": "Given that Canadian research, policy, and evidence mobilisation has been instrumental in developing teachers' beliefs in the value of assessment for learning in the last two decades as also evidenced by the findings in this study, there is discordance between teachers' and students' conceptions of assessment within the classroom. \n\nOur second research question, \"What are teachers' and students' approaches to grading?\", sought to understand whether differences exist in students' and teachers' perceived approaches to grading. The analysis of grading approaches demonstrated that teachers and students differed significantly on all four factors (social-emotional pressures for grade increases/changes, contextually based grading, achievement-based grading, and situational considerations for grade increases/changes). The discriminant analysis showed that contextually based grading and achievement-based grading had the strongest role in distinguishing teachers' and students' approaches to grading. Teachers indicated a stronger tendency to consider achievement the basis for grading, with grades largely representing achievement outcomes regardless of student effort, behaviour, and subject difficulty. Conversely, students preferred teacher grading to reflect effort, behaviour, and subject difficulty. Although teachers in this study showed less agreement with assessment for school and student accountability, they more strongly endorsed grading approaches to include only achievement. This finding is more promising given the continued call for achievement-based grading in the literature (Brookhart et al., 2016) and concerns that teachers include student behaviour, effort, and classroom habit in grading (Bonner & Chen, 2009;Brimi, 2011;Brookhart et al., 2016;Chen & Bonner, 2017;Sun & Cheng, 2014). On the other hand, students showed greater support for assessment for school and student accountability, as well as for including non-achievement factors such as effort, behaviour, and subject difficulty. Prior research found that students valued non-achievement factors such as effort, behaviour, and classroom habit in their grades (Gordon & Fay, 2010;Tippin, Lafreniere, & Page, 2012). Future research can investigate whether students' expectations of including non-achievement factors can drive teachers to include them in their actual grading practices, despite this study's finding that they believe otherwise.",
