      "snippet": {
        "text": "General Chemistry is a foundational gateway course for numerous majors and career paths, especially those offered in life sciences and engineering. 21,41 Disparities in academic performance, particularly in the introductory courses, contribute to the dropout rates of the underrepresented minorities pursuing STEM majors. 42 As shown in Figure S6, all CHE 98 participants declared majors within STEM disciplines. According to the survey findings, 68% of the students identify as premedical students, indicating a strong likelihood of enrolling in advanced chemistry courses. This underscores the importance of a solid understanding of General Chemistry for students' academic success. This study uniquely integrates academic efforts combined with social support to assist underrepresented groups and first-generation students from low-income backgrounds. We outline the interventions implemented in the CHE 98 co-class to address the disparities in student engagement and learning that arose after the transition from over a year of virtual learning to in-person instruction. Key strategies included the formation of small, interactive groups facilitated by a Teaching Assistant and Learning Assistant, lecture-based instruction, concept-focused problem-solving activities, practice exams, and mock assessments and the promotion of a flexible classroom environment tailored to the evolving needs of the student cohort. \n\nStruggles in General Chemistry are often tied to insufficient preparation at the high school level. The abstract nature of many chemical concepts contributes to students' perception of the subject as particularly challenging. −45 As a result, students enter General Chemistry with diverse academic backgrounds; while some have completed Advanced Placement (AP) Chemistry, others have little to no prior exposure. 21 This disparity leaves many students underprepared for the academic demands of postsecondary STEM degrees. Approximately 64% of students in the general population group achieved a passing grade, which is higher than those of both the control and CHE 98 groups. A possible explanation of this trend is that students in this group include students from well-represented populations and higher socioeconomic backgrounds, who often enter college with stronger academic preparations, as many might have attended well-funded private or public-school districts with better amenities and advanced coursework. Such advantages can significantly contribute to their academic performance. 24,46,47 During the COVID-19 pandemic, 85% of students enrolled in CHE 98 completed their high school chemistry courses in an online format.",
