        "text": "Teachers have traditionally assigned the same grades to coursework, group projects, attendance, and participation. This practice results in teachers evaluating all students' work using identical criteria, irrespective of their individual or group advancements (ET4-personal interview on 6 October 2023). \n\nThe expressions above suggest that it was common for teachers to assign similar grades to groups of students when evaluating their performance. This practice of applying standardized grades across different groups during assessments may compromise the precision and impartiality of evaluations for a diverse student body, diminishing the value of feedback that could pinpoint areas where students need improvement. Feedback systems should be structured to guide teachers on which students require further review and support, and which ones are prepared to advance to more challenging tasks. This approach ensures that assessment outcomes contribute to continuous learning progress for all students throughout the academic year, ultimately enhancing their academic success (Pudaruth et al., 2013). \n\nConsidering the teachers' practice of the sampled higher education institutions, the study argues, that public universities could not exert much effort in students' holistic development given that feedback is central to assessment for learning. Consistent to this point. Jones and Moreland (2005, p.5) claim, 'Assessment for learning is all about informing learners of their progress to empower them to take necessary actions'. The feedback culture should go to the level of identifying individual students' progress so that students learn based on their pace and performance. According to Muskin (2017), the ultimate purpose of CA is to determine how well teaching and learning are proceeding, to enhance classroom procedures, to make decisions about remediation, and to engage students. Traditional feedback practices like this one can potentially slow down efforts at active learning. To promote a learningcentered viewpoint, more informative and detailed feedback is required than the exclusive practice of marking as correct or incorrect (Faremi et al., 2023;Fl orez & Sammons, 2013). Thus, this study calls for all stakeholders at higher education institutions' attention to mainstream formative descriptive feedback.",
