        "text": "This provides learners with an opportunity to reflect on their learning process as self-assessment and adjust accordingly during self-regulated learning (Earl, 2013;Estaji & Safari, 2023;Lam, 2018). Assessment for learning (AfL), on the other hand, is the process of identifying a learning gap by seeking evidence, interpreting it, and performing appropriate instructional and learning interventions as needed (Black & William, 1998;Estaji & Kogani, 2024;Schellekens et al., 2021). This assessment notion focuses on learning and is \"part of the everyday practice by students, teachers, and peers that seeks, reflects upon, and responds to information from dialogue, demonstration, and observation in ways that enhance ongoing learning\" (Klenowski, 2009, p. 264). Finally, assessments of learning (AoL) are conducted at one time, typically after finishing a unit or at the end of a course or program, to collect evidence of learners' achievement for certification, accountability, or ranking purposes. Based on these explanations, assessments for teaching, as learning, and for learning are formative, whereas those of learning are summative. Examples of formative assessments are performance-based assessments, portfolios, self and peer assessments, and observations. End-of-course quizzes, and mid-term and final exams are summative assessments. However, the contemporary literature suggests using psychometrically based summative assessments for formative reasons (Lau, 2016;Latif & Wasim, 2022;Yan et al., 2021). \n\nIn the literature, we find varied perspectives on the connection between assessment notions AaL, AfL, and AoL. Some scholars believe that AaL can be typically considered a subsection of AfL (Earl, 2013;Lam, 2018); the others view AaL as the final stage in the progressive continuum aimed at enhancing assessment practices (Arnold, 2022;Carles, 2015;Torrance, 2017). Another perspective is that AaL, AfL, and AoL should be considered as interconnected elements w