      "snippet": {
        "text": "2012;Furtak et al., 2016).\n\nConsequently, formative assessment practices are an essential part of teachers' professional competence. Models of teachers' professional competence reflect this importance by defining facets that encompass formative assessment practices (Baumert & Kunter, 2013;Gess-Newsome, 2015;Park & Oliver, 2008). Some models regard assessment knowledge as a generic aspect of teachers' professional knowledge base (Gess-Newsome, 2015), others as a content specific aspect that is part of their pedagogical content knowledge (PCK; Baumert & Kunter, 2013;Park & Oliver, 2008). In both cases, judgement accuracy and feedback are measureable products of teachers' formative assessment practices that help to approximate their professional competence.\n\nIn general, only a few empirical findings exist that focus on teachers' formative assessment practices in the science subjects. Two studies from mathematics and science education show that the judgement of students' levels of achievement regarding subjectspecific concepts is comparably easy for in-service teachers (Heritage, Kim, Vendlinski, & Herman, 2009;Herman, Osmundson, & Silver, 2010). This is also true for preservice teachers from the two domains who had to analyse students' responses to probes referring to two chemical phenomena (Talanquer, Bolger, & Tomanek, 2015). In contrast, in-service and pre-service teachers struggle in the same context with the interpretation of students' ideas without any support (Furtak et al., 2016;Nussbaum, 1981;Talanquer et al., 2015). In addition, teachers often tend to make general remarks when giving feedback that do not support next steps in student learning.\n\nThe reported studies have two things in common. First, they focus mainly on formative assessment practices related to students' conceptual knowledge. Consequently, hardly no empirical findings exist that focus on pre-service teachers' formative assessment practices in the field of scientific inquiry. Compared to the assessment of students' conceptual knowledge, inquiry competences require different forms of interpreting students' ideas. Second, feedback is often not in the focus of pre-service teachers' practices. Both limitations are addressed in the two following sections by summarising the research regarding the quantity",
