        "text": "The area of research into the relationship between formative assessment and self-regulated\xa0learning is well established. Several studies have explored the connection between\xa0assessment and self-regulated learning, but the literature shows that there are still several lines of research to be explored, including examining which formative assessment\xa0practices actually help students improve their self-regulation skills and under which\xa0conditions. The paper presents a reasoned review of empirical studies, with experimental\xa0or quasi-experimental research design, that have examined the effect of formative\xa0assessment practices on self-regulated learning. The target population includes students\xa0from all levels of education. It was possible to classify two different ways of implementing\xa0formative assessment practices: a global approach and an analytical approach. Most of\xa0the reviewed studies recorded significant positive effects on self-regulation. The results\xa0and implications for future research are discussed.",
