      "snippet": {
        "text": "Despite these Vision and Change recommendations, many lower-level undergraduate biology courses continue to use summative assessments that evaluate lower-order thinking, often failing to evaluate students' understanding of scientific practices as they assess students' factual content knowledge instead of their conceptual under standing of biology (63). The shift toward more student-centered pedagogical practi ces has prompted a reevaluation of how student learning should be assessed (1). Although summative assessments such as midterms and final exams still dominate the approach to evaluation in introductory biology, an increasing number of instructors are adopting formative and alternative assessment to improve student learning (53,64,65). Unlike traditional high-stakes, summative assessments, low-stakes formative assessments, quizzing, and project-based assessments facilitate learning. Implementing multiple low-stakes formative assessments within an introductory biology classroom in the form of tests/quizzes that provide immediate feedback to students increases student learning gains and longitudinal knowledge transfer (65,66). Alternative assessment strategies that incorporate student reflections are gaining popularity, as they not only support improvements in student performance and learning outcomes but also promote the development of metacognitive awareness (67).",
