      "snippet": {
        "text": "Formative assessment practices have at their core assessing students' learning needs and adapting teaching and/or learning to meet these identified needs. Approaches to formative assessment may focus on the teacher or the students as the proactive agents in these processes. Classroom practices may also include both. However, detailed descriptions of the enactment of such multi-approach formative assessment practices in ordinary day-to-day authentic classrooms are rare, and empirical investigations identifying how such practices may work to support students' behavioural engagement and autonomous motivation are lacking. Consequently, little is known of how multi-approach formative assessment may affect students in terms of their behavioural engagement and type of motivation. \n\nThe present study addresses this gap in existing literature by analysing a multi-approach formative assessment practice to identify characteristics of the practice that have been argued to influence student motivational characteristics that affect behavioural engagement and autonomous motivation. The analysis of the classroom practice clearly shows that it can be considered to be a multi-approach formative assessment practice (RQ 1). In contrast to, for example, the classroom practices described by Antoniou and James (2014) in which no assessment control was passed over to the students, the classroom practice analysed in the present study includes a number of different activities engaging both teachers and students as proactive agents in the formative assessment practices. The study then contributes to the field of formative assessment and motivation by, to the best of our knowledge, being the first to identify characteristics of an authentic comprehensive multi-approach formative assessment that would influence motivational characteristics that positively affect students' type of motivation and engagement in learning activities (RQ 2). In Section 5.1 below we further discuss how the practice may have affected the students' motivation. In addition, the investigation of the effects of the implemented formative assessment practice on behavioural engagement and autonomous motivation (RQ 3) complement the very few existing empirical intervention studies that have examined changes in behavioural engagement (Wong, 2017) and autonomous motivation (Faber et al., 2017;F€ orster & Souvignier, 2014;Hondrich et al., 2018;Meusen-Beekman et al., 2016) (see Section 5.2 below for a comparison of the results).",
