          "disclaimer": "Notice: This snippet is extracted from the open access paper or abstract available at https://api.unpaywall.org/v2/10.1080/2331186X.2024.2371169?email=<INSERT_YOUR_EMAIL> or https://doi.org/10.1080/2331186X.2024.2371169, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."
        }
      },
      "snippet": {
        "text": "In these multi-approach practices, different approaches can support each other in facilitating students' behavioural engagement and autonomous types of motivation. For example, teachers' frequent assessment of students' understanding of relevant content, along with valid interpretations of their responses, facilitates opportunities to provide frequent feedback that clarifies for students the learning goals they have attained or can attain. Supporting students in taking on the role of proactive self-regulated learners includes supporting their self-assessment practice. Self-assessment in turn involves the students themselves frequently noticing continuing learning that may lead to more frequent experiences of enhanced self-efficacy and in turn increased behavioural engagement. Students' involvement as proactive agents and self-regulated learners in the formative assessment processes may be facilitated by teachers and students working together to attain a common interpretation of learning goals and success criteria. It is difficult for the students to be proactive learners if they are uncertain about the goals or the criteria for attaining them. \n\nThere does exist intervention studies in which a framework that can be considered to describe multiapproach formative assessment practices have been used to analyse and describe formative assessment practices (e.g., Antoniou & James, 2014;Kruiper et al., 2022;Wiliam et al., 2004). But some of these studies did not provide detailed descriptions of the analysed practises and did not investigate how the practices may have worked to affect engagement or autonomous motivation (e.g., Kruiper et al., 2022;Wiliam et al., 2004). Other studies found that the teachers did not engage in multi-approach formative assessment. For example, in the study by Antoniou and James (2014) the authors concluded that although their teachers had positive perceptions towards formative assessment, they implemented formative assessment practices mechanically, could not make explicit what the purpose of certain activities was or what would count as doing them well, and they were unwilling to pass over any assessment control to their students by engaging them in self-assessment or peer-assessment practices. \n\nThus, empirical studies describing in detail comprehensive multi-approach formative assessments carried out in ordinary day-to-day authentic classroom practice are rare, and empirical investigations identifying how such practices may work to support students' behavioural engagement and autonomous motivation are lacking.",
