      "snippet": {
        "text": "In this section we account for suggestions and evidence for whether, and how, formative assessment may positively affect student engagement and autonomous motivation. A few studies have empirically investigated the relationships between formative assessment and grade 1-12 students' behavioural engagement. Federici et al. (2016) found an association between students' perception of their teacher's formative assessment and aspects of behavioural engagement. Wong (2017) found a medium-size effect from an intervention in which a researcher taught the students formative assessment strategies, and Ghaffar et al. (2020) found some positive outcomes (no effect sizes provided) from a two-month formative assessment intervention on students' engagement in learning activities. In an eight-month intervention (N€ asstr€ om et al., 2021), where a teacher continuously gathered empirical evidence about students' learning and adapted feedback and learning activities to identified learning needs, small to medium sized effects were found from this formative assessment practice on student engagement. Some association studies have found relationships between single approaches to formative assessment and grade 1-12 students' autonomous types of motivation (Baas et al., 2020;Federici et al., 2016;Gan et al., 2019;Pat-El et al., 2012;Zhang, 2017). There also exist a few intervention studies, and they show mixed effects, from no effect to a nearly medium effect from formative assessment on students' autonomous forms of motivation (Faber et al., 2017;F€ orster & Souvignier, 2014;Hondrich et al., 2018;Meusen-Beekman et al., 2016;N€ asstr€ om et al., 2021). \n\nResearchers have provided different suggestions about how formative assessment may influence student motivational characteristics that affect motivation manifested as behavioural engagement in learning activities. Andrade and Brookhart (2020) draw on attribution theory and the effects of self-efficacy. They argue that good self-regulators make adaptive attributions of success and failure to reasons that are controllable by the students, and that such attributions are linked to persistence and effort (behavioural engagement) (Wolters et al., 2013).",
