        "text": "Fidelity in the delivery of core areas presented in ROBUST by teachers with their students is the goal of the PTT. The PTT should allow teachers to acquire new knowledge and skills that can be used consistently and appropriately in their own classroom practice (Greenberg et al., 2017;Joyce & Showers, 2002). The support system should provide opportunities for collective learning through meaningful reflections, observations, and discussions, including facilitators' provision of performance feedback as a critical element that contributes to success (Domitrovich et al., 2008). By training implementers in the core areas, the support system aims to increase consistency and reduce variability in the quality of intervention implementation (Domitrovich et al., 2008). \n\nBecause observation is the method encouraged to understand quality of delivery, which is often both time-consuming and resource-consuming, delivery is considered a demanding topic in implementation science (Domitrovich et al., 2019). Quality in delivery is linked to adaptation, understood as adding to or modifying processes and/or content that are planned and/or prescribed in the intervention manual (Berkel et al., 2011). However, adaptation might increase ownership for participants and sustain attendance and participation, thus contributing to behavior change (Berkel et al., 2011). Fidelity-consistent modifications that What can we learn from the transition to digital delivery for the design of future support systems for social and emotional learning interventions?",
