      "snippet": {
        "text": "Consequently, such competency-based assessments must lead to extensible and usable results. This usability can have direct and indirect, intended and unintended, short-term and long-term effects on learners and educational institutions (Baartman et al., 2007). \n\nThe quality of the developed model in terms of reproducibility of decision is rated at the highest level (Mdn ¼ 5), with experts showing moderate agreement (CQD ¼ 11.11%). This indicates a moderate dispersion of opinions, which is likely due to the fact that the model is based on the specific competency assessment principles of the 'KRU RAK THIN' program in remote, underprivileged areas. Therefore, this model is not directly transferable to other groups of student teachers, such as those not participating in the 'KRU RAK THIN' program. This is in line with DeLuca and Volante (2016) study on the challenges of promoting assessment models for learning in teacher education. Their study, which included an analysis of teacher experiences from five higher education institutions in Canada and the United States and a summary of relevant literature, identified three main limitations in promoting such assessment models: \n\n(1) time constraints, (2) model constraints, and (3) operational constraints within teacher education institutions. The researchers found that these constraints have a significant impact on teacher education curricula and have localized pedagogical implications. \n\nThe implementation of AfL in this research has also brought significant benefits to student learning and development. The continuous feedback provided through AfL practices has helped teacher students to better understand their learning pathways, recognize their strengths and work on their weaknesses, encouraging learners to take control of their own learning. Both teachers and students use the results of effective assessment to set learning goals, monitor performance and adjust teaching strategies. This includes redesigning learning processes and refining and correcting student performance (Brown et al., 2012;Brown & Frontiers Media SA, 2019;McLaren, 2012;Morris et al., 2021;Yigletu et al., 2023). This has improved student engagement and motivation as they are more aware of their progress and more engaged in their learning process. In addition, AfL has enabled teachers to tailor their teaching strategies to the different needs of students, improving the overall effectiveness of teaching.",
