      "snippet": {
        "text": "To monitor implementation fidelity, the study conducted three site visits per year, observing each class, including computer lab classes. Each control classroom was also observed three times per year. Visits took place in the fall, winter, and spring and were scheduled with teachers ahead of time. Given that the curriculum differed in treatment and control classrooms, observers were not blind to condition. Observations followed a rubric modeled on the developer's implementation guidelines, which covered important elements of the instructional design. During these visits, the observers also interviewed teachers about their implementation of the Cognitive Tutor curriculum and their instructional practices in both treatment and control classes. Prior to conducting the site visits, all classroom observers received three days of observation and interview training in addition to attending Carnegie Learning's 3-day training sessions for teachers. At each site visit, observers rated teachers' instructional practice on 18 dimensions in the treatment classes and on 9 of those dimensions in the control classes. Ratings in each dimension ranged from 1 (low) to 6 (high). Observers also recorded written justifications for each rating, which permitted subsequent data cleaning to improve consistency across raters and years. Even before data cleaning, Cohen's kappa for rater agreement ranged from 0.8 to 0.85 each year. \n\nWe summarized these ratings by creating four unweighted composite implementation measures from 16 of the 18 teaching practice dimensions. 3 We then modeled the relationship between these four composite measures and student outcomes. Two of the four composites, Learner (or Student)-Centered Practices and Student Participation, were observed in both treatment and control classes. The remaining two composite measures, Materials Fidelity and Computer Lab Use, are based on dimensions that were observed only in treatment classrooms. \n\nThe Learner-Centered Practices composite captures the extent to which a teacher, during a given class period and school year: assigns presentations, brings closure to activities, encourages students to make connections among ideas, and encourages students to collaborate with one another. We refer to these teaching practices as \"learner-centered\" (Cornelius-White & Harbaugh, 2010;McCombs, 2001) because they attend to students' cognitive assimilation of new concepts and control over their own learning. All are teaching strategies explicitly prescribed by Carnegie Learning, though they were also measured in nontreatment classrooms.",
